Here's the equivalent Java program that displays a greeting based on the current time of day:

```java
import java.time.LocalTime;

public class Greeting {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Get the current hour
        int hour = LocalTime.now().getHour();
        String greeting;

        if (hour < 12) {
            greeting = "Good Morning!";
        } else if (hour < 16) {
            greeting = "Good Afternoon!";
        } else if (hour < 22) {
            greeting = "Good Evening!";
        } else {
            greeting = "Good Night!";
        }

        // Display the greeting
        System.out.println("<html><body><h1>" + greeting + "</h1></body></html>");
    }
}
```

To use this program:

1. Copy the code above.
2. Paste it into a new file named `Greeting.java`.
3. Save the file.
4. Compile it with the command: `javac Greeting.java`
5. Run it with the command: `java Greeting`

This Java program will output the appropriate greeting message based on the current time of day when you run it in a command-line interface.

Here's a C program that displays a greeting based on the current time of day:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <time.h>

int main() {
    // Get the current time
    time_t now;
    time(&now);
    struct tm *current_time = localtime(&now);
    int hour = current_time->tm_hour;
    char *greeting;

    // Determine the appropriate greeting
    if (hour < 12) {
        greeting = "Good Morning!";
    } else if (hour < 16) {
        greeting = "Good Afternoon!";
    } else if (hour < 22) {
        greeting = "Good Evening!";
    } else {
        greeting = "Good Night!";
    }

    // Display the greeting
    printf("<html><body><h1>%s</h1></body></html>", greeting);

    return 0;
}
```

To use this program:

1. Copy the code above.
2. Paste it into a new file named `greeting.c`.
3. Save the file.
4. Compile it with a C compiler, for example, using gcc with the command: `gcc greeting.c -o greeting`
5. Run the compiled program with the command: `./greeting` (on Unix-like systems) or `greeting.exe` (on Windows)

This C program uses the system clock to get the current time and then determines the appropriate greeting message based on the current hour. It uses the `<time.h>` header to access functions that deal with time.
